result:
I found 5 issues in the one changed file, `calc.py`. The main one is an off-by-one in `total()`: it never adds the first element, and `mean()` inherits that. This review ran without a verification pass, as the recipe specifies. No CLAUDE.md applies to this repo, and nothing else in it calls `total` or `mean`.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "calc.py",
    "line": 3,
    "summary": "total() loops over range(1, len(items)), so it always skips items[0] and returns the wrong sum.",
    "failure_scenario": "total([5, 1, 2]) returns 3 instead of 8; total([7]) returns 0 instead of 7."
  },
  {
    "file": "calc.py",
    "line": 8,
    "summary": "mean() uses the broken total(), so every result is too small by items[0]/len(items).",
    "failure_scenario": "mean([10, 20, 30]) returns 50/3 ≈ 16.67 instead of 20.0; mean([4]) returns 0.0 instead of 4.0."
  },
  {
    "file": "calc.py",
    "line": 8,
    "summary": "mean() raises ZeroDivisionError on an empty sequence and has no guard or clear error for it.",
    "failure_scenario": "mean([]) raises an uncaught ZeroDivisionError, which crashes any caller that averages an empty result set."
  },
  {
    "file": "calc.py",
    "line": 3,
    "summary": "The rewrite drops support for arbitrary iterables: the old `for i in items` worked on any iterable, but the new code needs len() and indexing.",
    "failure_scenario": "total(x for x in data) raises TypeError: object of type 'generator' has no len(); total({1, 2, 3}) raises TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable. Both worked before this change."
  },
  {
    "file": "calc.py",
    "line": 1,
    "summary": "total() and mean() hand-roll what the builtin sum() and statistics.mean() already do.",
    "failure_scenario": "The manual index loop is exactly where the off-by-one crept in. `return sum(items)` and `statistics.mean(items)` (which raises a clear StatisticsError on empty input) remove both the bug and the extra code."
  }
]
